Assessment guidance

During the NEA, students will be expected to demonstrate their skills and understanding through 3 to 5 tasks in
the set assignment. Students are required to plan and design, create, test and review their Augmented Reality
(AR) model prototype solution for the particular context. Students must complete the assignment independently
therefore it is highly unlikely that the same AR model prototype solution is created by all students . Students
can use an augmented reality (AR) software development kit (SDK), or alternative software to complete the
assessment. For more information see Appendix C. If alternative software is used students must create a simulated
AR experience. Alternative software can include, but isn’t limited to, presentation software (e.g. PowerPoint,
Google Slides and Canva).

A simulated AR experience must show what AR would look like when it is working. This means using things
that move or change, such as simple animation, clicking or tapping to make something happen, and visual effects
including making objects appear closer or further away to look like they are in the same space as the real world.
The simulated AR experience must include the illusion of depth.

When an AR model prototype is made using an AR SDK, animation may be applied through built-in functionality
(e.g. via software tools). When using alternative software, such as presentation software students must create
these effects themselves using tools like animations, layers, size changes, and slide transitions. In both cases, the
aim is to show how virtual objects would look and behave in AR, not just to create a static set of slides.

Students must complete the full assignment before any evidence is marked. For Task 2, students must submit a
video or screen capture demonstrating the AR model prototype in use. Teachers must assess Task 2 using only
this recording. Marks based on alternative forms of evidence cannot be accepted.  Work to be submitted for
moderation must include the full recording. Moderators must see the recording and be confident the marks are
based on that recording.

Teaching content

 Assessment Guidance

1.1, 1.2 and 1.3

•	 Students must know and understand the different uses of Augmented Reality (AR)
in the different sectors, the different types of AR and the user interactions and the
devices used for AR to support their explanations when designing the AR product for
the assignment context.

•	 While this aspect is not directly assessed, students know and understand this topic

area in order to be able to complete the set assignment.
3.1, 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4 •	 Students must only create an AR model prototype meaning it  is a simple model

built and not a fully working AR product. Their AR model prototype will have limited
functionality to showcase how the fully working AR solution for a given context and
audience might look like. Think of it as a minimal viable product – just enough to
demonstrate to your client or customer what the full version will look like and how it
might function.

•	 Students can use an augmented reality (AR) software development kit (SDK), or

alternative software to be able to use the tools and techniques as listed in Topic 3
above to create, test and review their AR model prototype. For more information
see Appendix C. If alternative software is used students must create a simulated AR
experience.

4.1

•	 Students are required to carry out technical testing only as part of their assignment.

They must not share any aspect of their assignment or prototype. Therefore, students
must not carry out user testing.

