=== L3 Information, Data Types & Validation ===
id: bd0e5f6e-32e0-4339-9dc5-ab4e034559e4
slug: information-data-types-validation
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:04.513301+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Data vs information, the five data types, and how validation and verification tools reduce user errors.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Data and Information &mdash; Not the Same Thing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Students often use the words &ldquo;data&rdquo; and &ldquo;information&rdquo; as if they mean the same thing. They do not. <dfn class="term" data-def="Raw, unprocessed facts or figures that have no context on their own, such as the numbers 17, 19 and 22.">Data</dfn> is raw, unprocessed facts &mdash; numbers, letters or symbols that mean nothing without context. <dfn class="term" data-def="Data that has been processed and given context so that it is meaningful, such as the statement: the average daily temperature this week is 19 degrees.">Information</dfn> is data that has been processed and given meaning.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The key step is <strong>processing</strong>. Take the raw numbers 17, 19 and 22: on their own they are meaningless data. Process them &mdash; calculate the average, attach labels and a date &mdash; and you get the useful statement: &ldquo;The average daily temperature in Manchester this week was 19&deg;C.&rdquo; This distinction matters on the exam: an answer that simply says the numbers are &ldquo;information&rdquo; without describing the processing step will lose a mark.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the definition of data and the definition of information, then identify the one step that converts data into information.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Data is raw, unprocessed facts (e.g. &ldquo;17, 19, 22&rdquo;). Information is data that has been <strong>processed and given context</strong> to make it meaningful. Processing is the step that converts one into the other.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Data Types</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Choosing the correct data type is essential when designing any IT system. The five main data types are <dfn class="term" data-def="A data type consisting of a combination of letters and numbers, used for fields such as postcodes or product codes (e.g. AB12 3CD).">alphanumeric</dfn>, Boolean, date, numeric and text. Each has specific characteristics that make it suitable for different kinds of data.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Alphanumeric</strong> combines letters and numbers &mdash; ideal for postcodes (SW1A 1AA), reference numbers (INV-2024-001) and registration plates. <strong>Boolean</strong> stores only two values: true or false (or yes/no, 1/0). It suits any yes/no field, such as whether a customer has agreed to marketing emails. <strong>Date</strong> stores date values in a consistent format (e.g. DD/MM/YYYY) and allows date-based calculations such as working out how many days until an order is due.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Text</strong> stores any string of characters without treating them as numbers &mdash; used for names, addresses and descriptions where calculation is not needed. <strong>Numeric</strong> covers several sub-types: <em>integer</em> (whole numbers, e.g. quantity in stock), <em>real</em> or <em>decimal</em> (numbers with a decimal point, e.g. 4.99), <em>currency</em> (formatted decimal, e.g. &pound;4.99), and <em>percentage</em> (a decimal expressed as a percentage, e.g. 12.5%).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Validation vs Verification &mdash; The Critical Difference</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9"><dfn class="term" data-def="An automated check that data entered into a system is reasonable, in the correct format or within expected limits — it cannot check whether the data is factually correct.">Validation</dfn> is an automated check that data is <em>reasonable and in the right format</em>. It happens automatically as the user types or submits a form. For example, a validation rule might check that an age field contains a number between 0 and 120.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Crucially, validation cannot check whether data is <em>true</em>. If someone enters their age as 25 when they are actually 30, the validation rule accepts the value &mdash; it is a plausible number. Validation only catches data that is impossible or implausible.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11"><dfn class="term" data-def="A check that confirms data has been entered correctly by comparing two entries or having a person check the original source &mdash; used to catch transcription errors.">Verification</dfn> checks that data has been entered <em>correctly</em> by comparing it against the original source. The two verification methods are <strong>double entry</strong> (asking the user to type the same data twice, e.g. confirming a password) and <strong>manual checking</strong> (a person compares printed output against the original document). Verification catches transcription errors that validation cannot detect.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Data Validation Tools</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">There are nine named validation tools. A <strong>data type check</strong> ensures the entry matches the expected data type (e.g. rejects letters in a numeric field). A <strong>format check</strong> tests that data follows a specific pattern &mdash; for example, an <dfn class="term" data-def="A validation tool that forces data entry into a set pattern by only allowing certain characters in each position, such as LL99 9LL for a UK postcode.">input mask</dfn> for a UK postcode allows only the pattern AA99 9AA. A <strong>length check</strong> ensures the entry is not too short or too long (e.g. a PIN must be exactly 4 digits).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">A <strong>limited choice</strong> tool restricts the user to a predefined set of values. This includes <strong>drop-down lists</strong> (the user selects from a visible list), <strong>radio buttons</strong> (the user selects one option from a small set) and <strong>tick lists</strong> (the user checks one or more boxes). A <strong>lookup</strong> checks that the entry exists in a reference table &mdash; for example, checking that a customer ID exists in the customer database before an order is placed. A <strong>presence check</strong> ensures a required field is not left blank. A <strong>range check</strong> ensures a numeric value falls within acceptable limits (e.g. exam scores must be between 0 and 100).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Choosing the Right Data Type and Validation Tool</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Exam questions often give a scenario (e.g. a hospital booking system) and ask you to justify a data type or validation tool for a specific field. Work through the field&rsquo;s purpose: does it store numbers used in calculations? Use a numeric type. Does it need a specific pattern? Use a format check or input mask. Is it a yes/no field? Use Boolean. Must it come from a fixed list? Use a drop-down list or radio buttons.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Remember: validation is automatic (built into the form), verification is a human or repeated-entry check. A hospital patient&rsquo;s date of birth field would use a <em>date</em> data type, a <em>format check</em> to enforce DD/MM/YYYY, a <em>range check</em> to ensure it is not in the future, and might use <em>double entry</em> (verification) to confirm the nurse typed it correctly.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write down all nine validation tool names from memory. Then check how many you got right.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The nine validation tools are: data type check, format check, input mask, length check, limited choice (drop-down list / radio buttons / tick list), lookup, presence check and range check. These can appear in any exam scenario question.</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Data is raw unprocessed facts; information is data that has been processed and given context. The processing step is what converts one into the other.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The five data types are alphanumeric, Boolean, date, numeric (integer, real, decimal, currency, percentage) and text &mdash; each suits specific kinds of stored values.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Validation is an automated check that data is reasonable (nine named tools); verification checks data was entered correctly via double entry or manual checking. They serve different purposes and both reduce errors.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">The most common error on data/validation questions is confusing validation and verification. Use this quick test: validation is automated and checks whether data is <em>reasonable</em>; verification checks whether data was <em>entered correctly</em> (by double entry or manual checking). If a question uses the command word &ldquo;explain&rdquo;, you must include a connecting phrase such as &ldquo;because&rdquo; or &ldquo;this means that&rdquo; &mdash; listing two validation tools without explaining what each one does will only score partial marks.</p>

