=== L4 Data Collection, Storage & Testing ===
id: d8d48adb-daba-4cea-9d80-f3f4c1a28711
slug: data-collection-storage-testing
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:09.089798+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Primary and secondary data collection, storage devices, and how test data proves an IT product works correctly.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">How Data Is Collected</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before an IT system can process or store data, that data must be collected. The spec divides collection methods into two categories. <dfn class="term" data-def="Data collected first-hand by the person or organisation that needs it, such as through email, interview or online questionnaire.">Primary data</dfn> is gathered directly by the organisation: examples include sending an email survey, conducting an interview and publishing an online questionnaire. Primary data is current and specific to your needs, but it takes time and resources to collect.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3"><dfn class="term" data-def="Data collected by someone else for a different purpose, which is then used by another organisation, such as data from books, government statistics, magazines or websites.">Secondary data</dfn> has already been collected by someone else &mdash; examples include books, government statistics, magazines and websites. Secondary data is often free and immediately available, but it may be out of date, biased or not exactly matched to what you need. In an exam scenario, you may need to judge which collection method suits the described context and justify your choice.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Close this and list: name three primary data collection methods and three secondary data sources from the spec, then write one advantage of each category.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Primary data collection methods: email, interview, online questionnaire/survey. Secondary data sources: book, government statistics, magazine, website. Primary data is specific but costly to collect; secondary data is cheap and fast but may be outdated or not tailored to your needs.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Where Collected Data Is Stored</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Once data is collected, it must be stored. The spec separates storage into <strong>logical location</strong> (where the data appears to the user) and <strong>physical location</strong> (where it actually lives).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="Remote servers accessed over the internet to store and retrieve data, so files are available from any device with internet access.">Cloud storage</dfn> is the key logical location: data is stored on remote servers managed by a third-party provider and accessed via the internet. Cloud storage is scalable and accessible from anywhere, but it requires an internet connection and raises data security and privacy concerns.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Physical storage splits into <strong>internal</strong> and <strong>external</strong> devices. Internal devices sit inside the computer: the <strong>primary hard drive</strong> stores the OS, applications and user files; a <strong>network drive</strong> is a storage device shared across a local network, allowing multiple users to save to a central location. External devices are removable and portable: a <strong>portable HDD</strong> (hard disk drive) offers large capacity at low cost but is vulnerable to physical damage; a <strong>portable SSD</strong> (solid-state drive) is faster, lighter and more durable but more expensive per gigabyte; a <strong>portable USB flash drive</strong> (memory stick) is small and convenient for small files; a <strong>network-attached storage (NAS) device</strong> connects to a network and provides shared storage without needing a server; a <strong>portable wireless drive</strong> creates its own Wi-Fi hotspot so multiple devices can connect without cables.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Comparing Storage Devices</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">Exam questions often ask you to select a storage option for a given scenario. Think about: <em>capacity</em> (how much data?), <em>portability</em> (does it need to move between locations?), <em>speed</em> (how quickly does it need to be read/written?), <em>cost</em> and <em>security</em> (who else could access it?).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">A photographer backing up thousands of RAW image files needs high capacity &mdash; a portable HDD or NAS device is suitable. A student saving a single coursework document needs portability &mdash; a USB flash drive works. A business with ten employees needing shared access to the same files benefits from cloud storage or a NAS device so everyone accesses the latest version from any device.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write the name and one characteristic of each external storage device type: portable HDD, portable SSD, USB flash drive, NAS, portable wireless drive.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>There are five named external storage devices: portable HDD, portable SSD, NAS device, portable USB flash drive and portable wireless drive. Each has different capacity, speed, cost and portability characteristics &mdash; always match your choice to the scenario&rsquo;s needs.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Why Testing Matters</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Testing is essential because IT products that have not been properly tested can crash, produce incorrect outputs or create security vulnerabilities. The consequences of not testing include data loss, financial damage, reputational harm and, in safety-critical systems (such as hospital equipment or traffic management), potential harm to people. The spec requires you to understand why testing is needed, the effects of not testing, and the advantages and disadvantages of testing.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Test Data</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The spec identifies three types of test data. <dfn class="term" data-def="Data within the expected and acceptable range that the system should accept and process correctly, used to confirm normal operation.">Valid test data</dfn> is typical data the system should accept. <dfn class="term" data-def="Data at the boundaries of the acceptable range that the system should still accept — used to test whether boundary conditions are handled correctly.">Extreme test data</dfn> is at the very edge of what is acceptable (e.g. 0 and 100 on a 0&ndash;100 scale) &mdash; it should be accepted. <dfn class="term" data-def="Data that falls outside the acceptable range or is in the wrong format, which the system should reject, such as a letter entered in a numeric field.">Invalid (erroneous) test data</dfn> is data that should be rejected (e.g. &minus;5 on a 0&ndash;100 scale, or the word &ldquo;hello&rdquo; in an age field).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">A common mistake is treating extreme and invalid as the same. They are not: extreme values <em>pass</em> the check (they are valid, just at the boundary); invalid values <em>fail</em> the check. For example, on a ticket booking form where age must be 1&ndash;120: valid test data = 25; extreme = 1 and 120; invalid = 0, 121, &ldquo;twelve&rdquo;.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Technical Testing and User Testing</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Technical testing</strong> checks that all functions work as specified &mdash; for example, that a formula produces the correct output, that navigation links go to the correct page, and that validation rules reject invalid entries. It is carried out by the developer.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18"><strong>User testing</strong> checks that real users can accomplish tasks effectively without confusion. It uncovers usability problems that technical testing misses &mdash; for example, a button may work technically but be placed in a position that users find confusing. Feedback from user testing is used to refine the HCI design and improve accessibility.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Data can be collected through primary methods (email, interview, online questionnaire &mdash; original, specific, costly) or secondary sources (book, government statistics, magazine, website &mdash; fast, cheap, possibly outdated).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Storage options range from cloud (accessible anywhere, needs internet) through internal devices (primary hard drive, network drive) to five named external devices: portable HDD, portable SSD, NAS device, USB flash drive and portable wireless drive.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Test data has three types: valid (should be accepted), extreme (boundary &mdash; should be accepted), invalid/erroneous (should be rejected). Technical testing checks functionality; user testing checks usability.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">The three types of test data are a frequent source of exam errors. Remember: valid data should be accepted and processed correctly; extreme data is at the boundary but should still be accepted; invalid/erroneous data should be rejected. If a question asks you to &ldquo;describe&rdquo; each type, name it and give a specific example linked to the scenario &mdash; for instance, if the scenario mentions an age field for a cinema booking system, say &ldquo;valid: age 25; extreme: age 1 and age 100; invalid: &minus;1 or the word &lsquo;old&rsquo;&rdquo;. Avoid vague answers like &ldquo;a number&rdquo; without connecting it to the context.</p>

