=== L6 Prevention Measures & IT Legislation ===
id: 0d6e448b-2d22-4536-a281-5d5fc2dcaea2
slug: prevention-measures-it-legislation
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:12:59.775558+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Physical, logical and secure-destruction measures, plus five Acts every IT professional must understand.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Prevention Matters</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Knowing about cyber-security threats (from Lesson 5) is only half the picture. The other half is knowing how to stop them. Prevention measures fall into three categories: <strong>physical</strong> (controlling access to devices and buildings), <strong>logical</strong> (software-based controls) and <strong>secure destruction</strong> (safely disposing of data and devices). Each reduces a different kind of risk.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Physical Prevention Measures</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Physical measures stop unauthorised people from reaching hardware in the first place. <dfn class="term" data-def="Security devices that identify individuals using unique biological characteristics such as fingerprints, retina scans or facial recognition to grant or deny access.">Biometric devices</dfn> (fingerprint scanners, retina readers, facial recognition systems) grant access only to enrolled individuals &mdash; they are difficult to forge and eliminate the need for keys that can be lost or copied. <strong>Keypads</strong> require a PIN code to enter a secured area &mdash; quick to use but vulnerable if someone watches the code being entered (shoulder surfing). <dfn class="term" data-def="Radio-frequency identification — a system that uses radio waves to read data from a chip embedded in a card or tag, used for access control (e.g. staff ID cards) and asset tracking.">RFID</dfn> (Radio-Frequency Identification) is used in staff ID cards that grant access by being held near a reader &mdash; cards can be cloned if not properly protected. <strong>Firewalls</strong> in hardware form are dedicated devices placed between a network and the internet, inspecting all traffic and blocking suspicious data packets. <strong>Secure backups</strong> (physical offsite copies) ensure data can be recovered if hardware is damaged, stolen or destroyed.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Logical Prevention Measures</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Software-based controls that protect IT systems and data, including access rights, anti-virus software, encryption, firewalls and two-factor authentication.">Logical prevention measures</dfn> are enforced by software. <strong>Access rights and permissions</strong> ensure users can only view, edit or delete the files their role requires &mdash; a call-centre agent should not be able to access payroll files. <strong>Anti-virus and anti-malware software</strong> scans for known malware patterns and quarantines or removes threats. <strong>Usernames and passwords</strong> are the most basic logical control: they should be complex (mixed case, numbers, symbols) and unique per account.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="A security method that requires a user to provide two separate forms of identification before access is granted — typically something they know (password) plus something they have (a code sent to a phone).">Two-factor authentication (2FA)</dfn> adds a second layer: even if an attacker obtains a password, they also need the one-time code sent to the user&rsquo;s phone. <dfn class="term" data-def="The process of converting data into an unreadable format (ciphertext) using a key, so that only authorised parties with the correct key can read it.">Encryption</dfn> scrambles data in transit and at rest, so that intercepted data is useless without the decryption key. <strong>Secure backups</strong> in logical form means keeping encrypted digital copies in a separate location &mdash; automated cloud backups are a common approach. Software <strong>firewalls</strong> monitor incoming and outgoing traffic on a single device, blocking suspicious connections.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Secure Destruction of Data</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">When a device is retired or sold, simply deleting files is not enough &mdash; data can be recovered from a &ldquo;deleted&rdquo; drive using widely available software. Four named methods ensure data cannot be recovered. <strong>Data erasure</strong> overwrites every storage location with random data multiple times, leaving no trace of the originals. <strong>Data sanitation</strong> is a broader process of clearing a storage medium to a certified standard, often including overwriting and verification that nothing remains. <strong>Magnetic wipe</strong> (degaussing) uses a powerful magnet to disrupt the magnetic patterns on a traditional hard drive, rendering data unreadable &mdash; it does not work on SSDs or USB drives. <strong>Physical destruction</strong> (shredding, crushing or melting the storage medium) is the most absolute method; it is typically used for drives containing highly sensitive data where recovery must be provably impossible.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n9" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: name one physical, one logical and one secure-destruction measure, then explain in one sentence how each one mitigates a specific threat.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Physical measures (biometric devices, keypads, RFID, firewalls, secure backups) prevent unauthorised physical access. Logical measures (access rights, anti-virus, 2FA, encryption, firewalls, passwords, secure backups) prevent unauthorised software access. Secure destruction (data erasure, sanitation, magnetic wipe, physical destruction) ensures data on retired devices cannot be recovered.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">IT Legislation &mdash; Five Acts to Know</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The exam requires knowledge of five Acts: what each one makes illegal or requires, who it protects, and its implications for data, individuals and organisations. Detailed knowledge of specific sections or clause numbers is <em>not</em> required. Treat each Act at the level of purpose and implications.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A UK law that makes unauthorised access to computer systems, unauthorised access with intent to commit a further offence, and unauthorised modification of computer material criminal offences.">Computer Misuse Act</dfn> creates three criminal offences: (1) accessing a computer without authorisation, (2) accessing a computer with intent to commit a further crime, and (3) modifying computer data without authorisation (e.g. installing malware). It protects individuals and organisations from hackers and malware creators. The implication for organisations: they should maintain logs of access attempts to detect and prove unauthorised access.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A UK law that protects original creative works (software, music, images, video) from being copied, distributed or used without the creator's permission.">Copyright, Designs and Patents Act</dfn> protects original creative work &mdash; including software, images, music and written content &mdash; from being copied or distributed without permission. For IT, this means you cannot legally copy software, use images from the web without a licence, or resell a software product you did not create. Organisations must ensure they have valid licences for all software they use.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Data Protection Act, Freedom of Information Act and Health &amp; Safety at Work Act</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The <dfn class="term" data-def="UK law (incorporating UK GDPR principles) that sets rules for how personal data must be collected, stored, processed and shared — giving individuals rights over their own data.">Data Protection Act</dfn> governs how organisations collect, store, use and share personal data. Organisations must have a lawful reason for holding data, keep it accurate and secure, not retain it longer than necessary, and give individuals the right to access or correct their data. Failure to comply can result in regulatory fines. This Act directly supports cyber-security by requiring organisations to secure personal data against unauthorised access.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">The <strong>Freedom of Information Act</strong> gives the public the right to request information held by public bodies (government departments, schools, NHS trusts, local councils). Public bodies must respond within 20 working days. For IT, this means public sector organisations must store records in accessible formats and must have systems in place to locate, retrieve and release information on request &mdash; while withholding genuinely exempt information (such as commercially sensitive or personal data).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">The <strong>Health and Safety at Work Act</strong> requires employers to provide a safe working environment. In IT contexts, this includes: positioning screens to reduce glare, providing ergonomic chairs and keyboards to prevent repetitive strain injury, ensuring cables are managed to prevent tripping hazards, and offering regular breaks from screen use. Employees also have a responsibility to use equipment safely.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Close this and write one sentence for each Act: what it makes illegal or requires, and one implication for an IT organisation.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five Acts: Computer Misuse Act (unauthorised computer access illegal), Copyright, Designs and Patents Act (protects creative works from copying), Data Protection Act (governs personal data handling), Freedom of Information Act (public right to access public-body information), Health and Safety at Work Act (employer duty to provide safe IT working environment).</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Prevention measures split into physical (biometrics, keypads, RFID, hardware firewalls, backups), logical (access rights, anti-virus, 2FA, encryption, passwords, software firewalls, backups) and secure destruction (data erasure, sanitation, magnetic wipe, physical destruction).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The five Acts in scope: Computer Misuse Act, Copyright Designs and Patents Act, Data Protection Act, Freedom of Information Act, Health and Safety at Work Act. Know the purpose and implications for data, individuals and organisations &mdash; not the detailed legal clauses.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Legislation supports cyber-security by creating legal duties: organisations that fail to protect data face regulatory fines; individuals who access systems without permission commit criminal offences.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">Legislation questions most often use the command word &ldquo;describe&rdquo; or &ldquo;explain&rdquo;. For a 3-mark &ldquo;explain&rdquo; answer, state the purpose of the Act, explain what it requires an organisation to do, and link it to the scenario. For example: &ldquo;The Data Protection Act requires organisations to keep personal data secure [purpose]; this means the hospital must encrypt patient records and restrict access to authorised staff only [requirement]; if they fail to do this, they could face a regulatory fine and patients could have their medical data stolen [implication].&rdquo; Never write detailed clause numbers &mdash; the spec explicitly excludes detailed knowledge of the legislation.</p>

