=== L7 Digital Communications & Distribution ===
id: 98637ea0-8dd3-4bee-92e7-1adf8faaaba7
slug: digital-communications-distribution
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:12.771055+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 20

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Types of digital communication, the software and devices used to create them, and the channels that deliver them.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Digital Communications?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="Information created and delivered in digital formats, including audio, video, infographics, newsletters, presentations, social media posts and websites.">Digital communications</dfn> are the messages, content and information that organisations and individuals create and share using digital technology. The spec lists eleven types: audio, collaboration tools, leaflets, infographics, newsletters, presentations, reports, social media, video, Voice over Internet Protocol (VoIP) and websites. For each type, you must know its purpose, advantages, disadvantages and when it is the most suitable choice.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: name all eleven types of digital communication without looking, then mark how many you remembered.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The eleven communication types are: audio, collaboration tools, leaflet, infographics, newsletters, presentations, reports, social media, video, VoIP and websites. Each has a specific purpose that makes it the best choice in certain contexts but not others.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Key Communication Types and Their Purposes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Audio</strong> (podcasts, audio adverts, voice messages) communicates without visuals &mdash; useful for on-the-go consumption (commuters, gym users) but cannot convey diagrams or visual data. <strong>Video</strong> combines moving images, audio and text &mdash; highly engaging and suited to demonstrations, tutorials and marketing, but expensive and time-consuming to produce and requires fast internet to stream. <strong>Infographics</strong> present data and statistics visually using charts, icons and minimal text &mdash; excellent for making complex data memorable and shareable on social media, but oversimplification can reduce accuracy.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Digital tools that allow multiple people to work on the same document or project in real time from different locations, such as shared online documents or project management software.">Collaboration tools</dfn> enable teams to work together remotely in real time &mdash; editing shared documents, tracking tasks or running virtual meetings. <strong>VoIP</strong> (Voice over Internet Protocol) transmits voice calls over the internet rather than a traditional phone network &mdash; it is cheaper for international calls but depends on a stable internet connection. <strong>Social media</strong> enables rapid, two-way communication with large audiences at low cost, but content can spread inaccurate information quickly and organisations lose some control over how their message is interpreted.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Newsletters</strong> deliver regular updates directly to subscribers &mdash; a targeted audience who have opted in &mdash; making them effective for maintaining customer relationships, but open rates decline if content is not relevant. <strong>Websites</strong> provide a permanent, accessible home for information that any user can find at any time. <strong>Leaflets</strong> in digital form are PDF documents or email attachments that work like printed leaflets but can be distributed cheaply via email or download. <strong>Presentations</strong> support verbal communication with slides &mdash; effective in meetings or events but require a presenter to add context. <strong>Reports</strong> provide structured, detailed information for decision-making &mdash; less engaging but highly credible for formal audiences.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Software and Digital Devices Used for Digital Communications</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Desktop publishing (DTP) software</strong> is designed for creating visually rich documents like leaflets, infographics, newsletters and reports. It offers precise control over layout, typography, images and colour. <strong>Standard office applications</strong> (word processors, presentation tools, spreadsheets) are more widely available and familiar but offer less design control than DTP software. For the exam, you need to select and justify the most appropriate software for a given scenario.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">The five named digital devices are: <strong>smartphone</strong> (portable, always connected, suited to social media, messaging, VoIP and consuming audio/video content on the move); <strong>smart TV</strong> (large screen, suited to streaming video and presenting visual content to groups); <strong>PC/laptop</strong> (full keyboard and screen, suited to creating detailed reports, presentations and websites); <strong>tablet</strong> (portable, touchscreen, suited to presenting content in meetings or accessing collaboration tools); <strong>smartboard</strong> (interactive display used in classrooms and meeting rooms for presentations and collaborative tools). Select the most suitable device by matching its characteristics to the context described in the scenario.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Distribution Channels and Connectivity</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11"><dfn class="term" data-def="The method used to deliver digital communications to an audience, such as email, cloud services, messaging apps, mobile apps, multimedia platforms, VoIP services or websites.">Distribution channels</dfn> are how content reaches its audience. The seven named channels are: cloud (content stored and accessed via online platforms), email (direct, targeted delivery to known recipients), messaging (instant text-based communication for individuals or groups), mobile apps (branded app experiences), multimedia platforms (video/audio hosting and streaming), VoIP (internet voice/video calls) and websites (publicly accessible, searchable).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Each channel works over a connectivity method. <strong>Wired</strong> connections (Ethernet cable) offer high speed and reliability but require physical infrastructure. <strong>Wi-Fi</strong> provides wireless connectivity within a building. <strong>Bluetooth</strong> connects devices at short range (typically up to 10 metres) and is used for peripherals and file transfer. <strong>4G/5G</strong> mobile networks allow internet access away from Wi-Fi hotspots. <strong>Mobile Wi-Fi hotspots</strong> use a mobile data connection to create a portable Wi-Fi network that other devices can join. Selecting the right connectivity method depends on range, speed, cost and whether the user is in a fixed or mobile location.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Matching Communication to Audience Demographics</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">The spec requires you to match a communication type, distribution channel and connectivity method to a specific audience demographic. The four demographic factors are: <strong>accessibility</strong> (can the audience use the format &mdash; does it need assistive technology, large text, or caption support?), <strong>age</strong> (younger users may prefer social media and mobile apps; older users may prefer email or printed leaflets), <strong>gender</strong> (some platforms attract different audience profiles &mdash; relevant for targeted marketing) and <strong>location</strong> (a rural audience with limited broadband may need content that works over mobile data; an international audience may need VoIP rather than phone calls).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">For example: a charity campaign targeting 18&ndash;25 year olds in urban areas to recruit volunteers would suit social media posts (communication type), mobile app distribution (channel) over 4G/5G (connectivity) &mdash; because the demographic is mobile-first, uses social platforms heavily, and may be on the move when they see the campaign. An annual financial report for company shareholders would suit a formal report (communication type), emailed as a PDF (channel) over wired broadband (connectivity) &mdash; because the audience expects detailed, credible data and likely accesses it on a desk computer.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: name the seven distribution channels and the five connectivity methods, then check your list against the lesson.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The seven distribution channels are: cloud, email, messaging, mobile apps, multimedia, VoIP and websites. The five connectivity methods are: wired, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, 4G/5G and mobile Wi-Fi hotspots. Always match your choice to the audience&rsquo;s location, device, age and accessibility needs.</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Eleven digital communication types (audio, collaboration tools, leaflet, infographic, newsletter, presentation, report, social media, video, VoIP, website) each have specific purposes, advantages and disadvantages &mdash; the right choice depends on the context and audience.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Software (DTP or standard office) and five digital devices (smartphone, smart TV, PC/laptop, tablet, smartboard) are used to create and present communications &mdash; match the device to the scenario&apos;s requirements.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Seven distribution channels (cloud, email, messaging, mobile apps, multimedia, VoIP, websites) deliver content over five connectivity methods (wired, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, 4G/5G, mobile Wi-Fi hotspot) &mdash; audience demographics (accessibility, age, gender, location) determine the best combination.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">For the 8-mark &ldquo;create&rdquo; question in this topic, you may be asked to sketch a diagram showing how a digital communication reaches its target audience &mdash; for example, mapping the communication type, software used, device, distribution channel and connectivity. Label every box and arrow clearly, and make sure every element connects back to the scenario. A common mistake is drawing a generic diagram without naming the specific communication type, device or channel the scenario mentions &mdash; this loses the scenario-link marks. For extended response questions, justify your choices: &ldquo;I chose email because the audience is over 50, so they are more likely to use email than social media.&rdquo;</p>

