=== L10 Operating Systems and Digital Platforms ===
id: 354e675f-7fa4-4c3b-b2df-34af32694fd0
slug: operating-systems-and-digital-platforms
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:09.304725+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Windows, macOS, iOS, Android, Chrome OS, Linux and Unix — how each OS shapes HCI design decisions.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Does an Operating System Do?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before you can design or use an HCI, something must manage the hardware, run the software and provide the interface layer between the user and the machine. That something is the <dfn class="term" data-def="Software that manages a device's hardware resources, provides a platform for other software to run on, and presents the interface through which users interact with the device.">operating system (OS)</dfn>. Every digital device &mdash; from a smartphone in your pocket to a server in a data centre &mdash; runs an operating system. The OS performs three core jobs: it manages hardware resources (telling the processor, RAM and storage when and how to be used), it runs software applications, and it presents the HCI through which users interact with the device.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">This topic is flagged by examiners as content that is not covered in the coursework units &mdash; which means students who revise only from their NEA projects often miss it entirely. The exam asks you to name specific operating systems, describe their key characteristics and explain how the OS choice shapes the HCI design.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Desktop and Laptop Operating Systems</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><dfn class="term" data-def="A family of desktop and laptop operating systems developed by Microsoft; the dominant OS in business and educational environments, known for its Start menu, taskbar and wide software compatibility.">Windows</dfn> is the dominant desktop OS in most business and educational environments. Its HCI is built around a taskbar at the bottom of the screen, a Start menu for launching applications and a windowed interface where multiple apps run simultaneously in resizable panels. Designers building for Windows can expect users to interact via mouse and keyboard, and to expect familiar navigation patterns such as right-click context menus.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Apple's desktop and laptop operating system; known for its Dock navigation bar, macOS-specific design guidelines (Human Interface Guidelines), and tight integration with Apple hardware.">Apple macOS</dfn> runs exclusively on Apple&rsquo;s own hardware. Its HCI uses a Dock at the bottom of the screen, a persistent menu bar at the top and a design language emphasised in Apple&rsquo;s Human Interface Guidelines. macOS designers target a user base that expects visually polished, consistent interfaces with smooth animations. Software compatibility is narrower than Windows but the hardware and software ecosystem is tightly controlled.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="An open-source family of operating systems built on the Linux kernel; freely available and widely used on servers, supercomputers and developer machines. Ubuntu is a popular user-friendly distribution.">Linux (and Ubuntu)</dfn> is open-source and free. Ubuntu is a widely used distribution designed to be accessible to non-technical users. Linux HCIs vary significantly: some use graphical desktops resembling Windows; others are primarily command-line interfaces used by developers and system administrators. Because Linux can be configured so freely, HCI designers targeting Linux environments must account for a wide range of possible interface configurations.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="One of the oldest operating systems, developed at Bell Labs; macOS evolved from Unix (via BSD/NeXT); Linux is a separately written Unix-like reimplementation, not a direct Unix descendant. Today Unix itself is used primarily in enterprise servers and academic systems.">Unix</dfn> is the historical foundation from which macOS evolved (via Apple's acquisition of NeXT, which was BSD-based). Linux is a separately written, Unix-inspired operating system — it is Unix-compatible but was not derived from Unix source code. Today it is found mainly in enterprise-grade server environments and academic institutions. Unix systems are typically operated via command-line interfaces, so they are not usually the target environment for consumer-facing HCI design &mdash; but the exam requires you to know it exists and its primary use context.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><dfn class="term" data-def="Google&rsquo;s lightweight operating system designed primarily for Chromebook laptops; almost all functionality runs through the Chrome browser and web-based applications rather than locally installed software.">Chrome OS</dfn> is Google&rsquo;s lightweight operating system found on Chromebook devices, popular in schools. Its HCI is almost entirely browser-based: apps run in Chrome tabs rather than as locally installed programs. This has significant design implications &mdash; Chrome OS users expect web applications, and offline functionality is limited. Designers building for Chrome OS must prioritise responsive web design and cloud-based workflows.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name all eight operating systems from the specification. For each, write the device type it is most associated with (e.g. desktop, mobile, server).">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The eight operating systems named in the specification are: <strong>Windows</strong> (business/education desktops), <strong>Apple macOS</strong> (Apple hardware), <strong>Apple iOS</strong> (iPhone/iPad), <strong>Android</strong> (most smartphones and tablets), <strong>Chrome OS</strong> (Chromebook laptops), <strong>Ubuntu</strong> (user-friendly Linux distribution), <strong>Linux</strong> (servers, developers), <strong>Unix</strong> (enterprise servers, academic systems).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Mobile Operating Systems</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="Apple&rsquo;s mobile operating system for iPhone and iPad; a closed ecosystem where all apps must be approved by Apple and distributed through the App Store.">Apple iOS</dfn> is a closed ecosystem &mdash; Apple controls exactly which apps can be installed, requiring all apps to go through the App Store and pass Apple&rsquo;s review process. HCI design for iOS follows Apple&rsquo;s Human Interface Guidelines closely: consistent navigation patterns, specific button sizes and placement conventions, and design expectations around gestures (swipe up to go home, long press for options). The closed ecosystem means fewer device variants to design for, but developers must follow Apple&rsquo;s rules.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13"><dfn class="term" data-def="Google&rsquo;s open-source mobile operating system; used on the vast majority of smartphones and tablets from many different manufacturers.">Android</dfn> is open-source and used on devices from many different manufacturers. This makes it the most widely used mobile OS globally. However, it creates a design challenge: Android runs on devices with hugely varying screen sizes, processor speeds and display resolutions. A designer targeting Android must test across multiple device types and plan for responsive layouts that adapt to different dimensions. The Google Play Store distributes Android apps, but the approval process is less restrictive than Apple&rsquo;s.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How OS Choice Shapes HCI Design Decisions</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">The OS creates the environment in which an HCI must operate &mdash; and each OS brings its own conventions, constraints and user expectations. On <strong>Windows</strong>, users expect right-click menus, resizable windows and keyboard shortcuts. On <strong>iOS</strong>, users expect swipe gestures, a consistent bottom navigation bar and no right-click functionality. On <strong>Linux</strong>, power users may prefer a command-line interface for speed and control. On <strong>Chrome OS</strong>, the interface must work primarily within a browser window.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Designing against the OS conventions produces poor HCI. An app that places navigation at the top of the screen on Android (where users expect it at the bottom) creates confusion. An interface that relies on hover effects will not work on any touch-based OS. Knowing the OS is therefore not optional in HCI design &mdash; it determines the interaction vocabulary the designer must use.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Digital Platforms</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Operating systems run software &mdash; and the software environment the HCI is designed for is called the <dfn class="term" data-def="The software environment or application type within which an HCI is designed to operate, such as a database, mobile app, spreadsheet or website.">digital platform</dfn>. The four digital platform types named in the specification are: <strong>database</strong>, <strong>mobile app</strong>, <strong>spreadsheet</strong> and <strong>website</strong>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18">A <strong>database</strong> platform presents data in structured tables and forms. The HCI for a database typically includes search fields, data entry forms, filter controls and tabular displays. Users are often data professionals rather than general consumers. A <strong>mobile app</strong> is installed on a smartphone or tablet and uses the device&rsquo;s touchscreen conventions. Its HCI must use large tap targets, limited navigation depth and mobile-specific interaction patterns. A <strong>spreadsheet</strong> platform (such as a desktop application or web-based tool) presents data in a grid of cells. HCI features include formula input, cell formatting and chart generation &mdash; designed for numeric analysis rather than casual browsing. A <strong>website</strong> runs in a browser and must be designed to work across different browsers, screen sizes and operating systems simultaneously. Responsive design &mdash; the ability to adapt the layout to different screen widths &mdash; is essential.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Matching Platform to Context in Exam Answers</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Exam questions on digital platforms often ask you to select and justify a platform for a scenario. Work through the user and task. A local GP surgery wanting to record patient appointment data with search and filter functionality &rarr; database (structured data, professional users). A national retailer wanting customers to browse and buy products on their phones &rarr; mobile app (touchscreen interaction, push notifications, stored payment details). A finance department needing to model budget forecasts &rarr; spreadsheet (formula-based calculations, chart output). A charity wanting to reach the widest possible audience with an information campaign &rarr; website (works on any device/OS without installation).</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n20" data-revision-tip="Close this and write one sentence for each of the four digital platforms: what type of data or task it is designed for, and one HCI design feature that is specific to that platform.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four digital platforms are: <strong>database</strong> (structured data entry and retrieval, form-based HCI), <strong>mobile app</strong> (touchscreen interaction, large tap targets, installed on device), <strong>spreadsheet</strong> (grid-cell numerical data, formula input, charts), <strong>website</strong> (browser-based, responsive across devices and OS, no installation needed).</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The eight operating systems named in the specification are Windows, macOS, iOS, Android, Chrome OS, Ubuntu, Linux and Unix &mdash; each has a primary use context and its own HCI conventions that designers must follow.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">iOS is a closed ecosystem controlled by Apple; Android is open-source and runs across a wide range of device types. Both shape the interaction patterns, navigation conventions and design guidelines a developer must follow.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Digital platforms (database, mobile app, spreadsheet, website) determine the structure and style of the HCI &mdash; each platform is suited to different types of tasks and users, and exam questions require you to justify which platform fits a given scenario.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">Operating system questions frequently use &ldquo;explain&rdquo; or &ldquo;describe&rdquo;. Naming an OS without linking it to HCI design impact scores only partial marks. Always connect the OS to the interaction patterns it creates: for example, &ldquo;iOS uses a closed ecosystem, meaning designers must follow Apple&rsquo;s guidelines, which requires consistent button placement and swipe gesture conventions throughout the app.&rdquo; For digital platform questions, justify your choice by linking the platform&rsquo;s characteristics to the specific user need in the scenario &mdash; avoid vague answers like &ldquo;a website is easy to use.&rdquo;</p>

