=== L11 Choosing Devices and Distribution Channels for an Audience ===
id: 4dc82838-0a74-4f4b-94a8-d65a63a208b3
slug: choosing-devices-and-distribution-channels-for-an-audience
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:12:55.078876+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 27

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Smartphones, tablets, smartboards and PCs matched to audience age, location and accessibility needs.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why the Audience Drives Every Device Decision</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Choosing a digital device is not just about picking the newest or cheapest option. Every digital communication needs to reach a specific audience &mdash; and that audience has needs, preferences and constraints that determine which device and channel will actually work for them. The spec area covered by this lesson appears directly in Section B scenario questions, where you are asked to recommend a device and justify your choice for a named audience. Getting this right requires a two-step move: identify the audience characteristic, then link it to the specific device or channel feature that addresses it.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Digital Devices &mdash; Five Types to Know</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The five digital devices in the specification are: <dfn class="term" data-def="A handheld mobile device that combines phone, internet access and app functionality; typically 5–7 inches, always connected, touch-operated.">smartphone</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="A large-screen interactive display (typically 55–86 inches) mounted on a wall or stand, designed for group presentations and collaborative use in classrooms or boardrooms.">smartboard</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="A personal computer or laptop; a desktop or portable device with a full-size keyboard, mouse and screen, optimised for productivity, data entry and complex software tasks.">PC/laptop</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="A portable touchscreen device larger than a phone (typically 7–13 inches) but smaller than a laptop; suited to media consumption, creative tasks and light productivity on the go.">tablet</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="A large-screen television set with internet connectivity, app support and, in some models, voice or remote control interaction.">smart TV</dfn>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">A <strong>smartphone</strong> is the most portable and personal device. It is always connected, fits in a pocket and is operated by touch. It is ideal for reaching individuals on the move &mdash; delivery notifications, banking alerts, social media campaigns targeting commuters. Its small screen limits complex data presentation, and it requires mobile-optimised content. A <strong>tablet</strong> offers a larger touchscreen than a phone without the full keyboard of a laptop. It suits media consumption, educational apps for children, and front-of-house uses such as restaurant ordering systems or museum interactive exhibits. A <strong>PC or laptop</strong> is the standard device for productivity-focused, data-heavy tasks: managing spreadsheets, writing long documents or running complex software. Users expect keyboard and mouse input, multi-window workflows and full-featured applications. A <strong>smartboard</strong> is designed for group use from a distance. It is fixed to a wall or stand, uses touch or stylus interaction, and must display content that is large enough to read across a room. It is best suited to classroom teaching, boardroom presentations and collaborative group workshops. A <strong>smart TV</strong> is designed for passive consumption at distance &mdash; video content, streaming services, and in some contexts public information displays. Interaction is typically via remote control or voice, not touch.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Device Advantages and Disadvantages at a Glance</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Smartphone:</strong> advantages &mdash; portable, always connected, push notifications reach users instantly, wide adoption. Disadvantages &mdash; small screen limits complex content, requires mobile-specific design, may be distracting in formal settings.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Tablet:</strong> advantages &mdash; larger screen than smartphone, portable, touch-operated, good for visual or interactive content. Disadvantages &mdash; less portable than a phone, limited productivity for text-heavy tasks, battery life varies.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>PC/laptop:</strong> advantages &mdash; large screen, full keyboard and mouse, powerful for complex tasks. Disadvantages &mdash; less portable, not suitable for on-the-move contexts, requires a desk or stable surface.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Smartboard:</strong> advantages &mdash; large group-visible display, supports collaborative annotation, good for educational and boardroom settings. Disadvantages &mdash; fixed in location, expensive, requires the audience to be physically present.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Smart TV:</strong> advantages &mdash; large display for shared viewing, familiar to most households, suitable for video-based communications. Disadvantages &mdash; limited interactivity, remote control input is imprecise, not suitable for data-heavy or text-heavy content.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Audience Demographics &mdash; Four Factors</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The spec requires you to assess device and channel suitability against four audience demographic factors: <dfn class="term" data-def="Accessibility needs relating to physical, visual, hearing or cognitive differences that affect how a person can interact with a digital device or interface.">accessibility</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="The age group of the target audience, which affects device familiarity, physical dexterity, content preferences and typical patterns of device ownership.">age</dfn>, gender and <dfn class="term" data-def="The geographical location of the audience, including whether they are urban or rural, which affects internet connectivity, device availability and channel reach.">location</dfn>.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write two sentences for each demographic factor: what it means, and one example of how it would change your device or channel recommendation.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four audience demographics are: <strong>accessibility</strong> (motor, visual, hearing and cognitive needs), <strong>age</strong> (children, working adults, older adults), <strong>gender</strong> (design, language and platform preferences may vary), <strong>location</strong> (urban vs rural broadband, regional device ownership patterns). Always link the demographic to a specific design or device feature in your answer.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Accessibility</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Accessibility covers four types of need. <strong>Motor accessibility</strong> affects how a person physically operates a device &mdash; someone with limited hand mobility may struggle with small touchscreen tap targets and benefit from a voice-controlled interface or large-button keyboard. <strong>Visual accessibility</strong> affects how a person sees the screen &mdash; someone with low vision needs large text, high contrast colours and screen-reader compatibility. This affects device choice (a large monitor over a small phone screen) and channel choice (a website with adjustable text size over a fixed-format PDF). <strong>Hearing accessibility</strong> affects audio-based content &mdash; a video delivered via smart TV needs subtitles or captions for deaf or hard-of-hearing users; a VoIP call is unsuitable for someone who cannot hear clearly. <strong>Cognitive accessibility</strong> affects how easily a person understands and navigates content &mdash; complex menus, long paragraphs and jargon-heavy text may be barriers for users with learning difficulties or limited IT literacy.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Age</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Younger children are typically tablet users &mdash; the larger touch targets and simplified app interfaces on tablets suit developing motor skills, and tablets are common in primary school classrooms. Teenagers and young adults are predominantly smartphone users with high digital literacy. Working adults use a mix of PC/laptop (workplace productivity) and smartphone (personal communication). Older adults may have lower digital literacy, prefer larger text and simpler navigation, and be less likely to have high-speed home broadband &mdash; a phone call or printed material may sometimes be more accessible than a digital channel for this demographic.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Gender and Location</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Gender demographics can influence platform preferences and design choices, though these vary widely and must not be over-generalised. In the exam, reference to gender demographics is typically about ensuring a design does not inadvertently exclude or alienate a portion of the audience &mdash; for example, using inclusive imagery and neutral language. Location is a more concrete demographic factor: urban users typically have access to high-speed broadband and 4G or 5G mobile coverage, making data-heavy channels (HD video streaming, large web applications) viable. Rural users may have slower or more limited connectivity, making lightweight channels &mdash; email, SMS-based messaging, or downloadable content that works offline &mdash; more reliable choices.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Exam Scenario: Recommending a Device and Channel</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20"><strong>Scenario:</strong> A national charity wants to promote a fundraising campaign to retired adults aged 65+ across rural areas. Which device and distribution channel would you recommend?</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Device:</strong> A PC or laptop, or a large-screen smart TV, would be more suitable than a smartphone. Older adults are less likely to own a smartphone and may have reduced motor dexterity making small touch targets difficult. A TV screen is familiar to this demographic and requires only remote control interaction. <strong>Channel:</strong> Email is a strong choice because it works on slow broadband connections (rural location), is widely used by retired adults who may check email on a PC, does not require app installation and can include large-text formatted content. A mobile app would be a poor choice &mdash; lower smartphone ownership in this demographic, potential difficulty installing and navigating apps.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n22">The exam-scoring move is always the same: state the demographic characteristic &rarr; link it to a device or channel feature &rarr; explain why that match is suitable (or why the alternative is unsuitable). A one-word answer such as &ldquo;email&rdquo; without justification scores zero on a 3-mark question.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n23" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write a two-sentence device recommendation for each demographic: (1) young children, (2) rural adults with limited connectivity, (3) someone with visual accessibility needs. Justify each choice.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The scoring pattern for device/channel recommendation questions is: <strong>audience characteristic &rarr; device/channel feature &rarr; justification</strong>. For example: &ldquo;The audience is older adults with reduced motor dexterity [demographic] &mdash; a smart TV with remote control interaction is suitable [device] because it avoids small tap targets that are difficult to use accurately [justification].&rdquo;</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n25">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">The five digital devices are smartphone, smart TV, PC/laptop, tablet and smartboard &mdash; each has a specific use context, interaction method and audience fit that must be matched to the scenario in the exam.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">The four audience demographic factors are accessibility (motor, visual, hearing, cognitive), age, gender and location &mdash; each directly affects which device and distribution channel is most suitable.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">Every device or channel recommendation must follow the scoring pattern: demographic characteristic &rarr; device or channel feature &rarr; justification. Naming the device without linking it to the audience characteristic scores only partial marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p>The most common examiner complaint is that students describe what a device is rather than why it suits the audience. For example: &ldquo;The audience is rural adults [location demographic] &mdash; email is a suitable distribution channel [channel] because it works on slow broadband connections that are common in rural areas and does not require a high-speed connection to receive [justification].&rdquo; Answers that just say &ldquo;email is fast and easy to use&rdquo; without referencing the demographic will score poorly.</p>

