 },
 {
  "text": "A hospital is designing an HCI for a patient monitoring system used in an intensive care ward. Sketch a comparison of gesture, touch and voice as interaction methods for this context, including advantages, disadvantages and a justified recommendation for each use case in the ward.",
  "type": "8 marks — Create (Sketch a Design)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8): sketch or structured comparison addresses all three methods in the hospital ICU context. Gesture: may be impractical for medical staff wearing gloves (reduced accuracy on capacitive screens) (1); suitable for navigating large data displays where swipe navigation aids review of patient history (1). Touch: direct and fast for selecting patient profiles and inputting readings (1); hygiene concern — touchscreens in clinical settings must be regularly cleaned; large, clearly labelled targets essential to avoid errors (1). Voice: ideal for hands-free operation when gloved or otherwise occupied (1); unsuitable in noisy ward environments where multiple alarms and conversations cause recognition errors; privacy risk if patient information is spoken aloud (1). Recommendation: justified selection of touch for routine data entry (clean screen protocols, large buttons) plus voice for hands-free alarm dismissal only, with gesture for secondary navigation (1). Explanation of why no single method is universally best in this context (1).\nSecure (5–6): two or three methods addressed with context-specific justification; recommendation present.\nDeveloping (3–4): methods listed with generic advantages/disadvantages, limited hospital context.\nEmerging (1–2): one method addressed or general description with no context.\n0: no creditworthy content."
